/* ============================================================================
   payback-period.epher -- how many years until a project's cash flows
   recover its cost, ignoring the time value of money

   The payback period adds up the yearly inflows until the cumulative total
   reaches the initial outflow. The table below carries the cumulative total
   year by year; a packed while loop then counts the full years and the
   final print adds the exact fraction of the next year (the builtin npv
   knows no payback, so the recurrence is written out). Source: standard
   capital-budgeting practice; see Brealey and Myers, Principles of
   Corporate Finance, chapter 5.

   Accuracy: the cumulative rows and the payback year are exact. The plain
   payback ignores the time value of money; the discounted version is the
   sibling script discounted-payback-period.epher.

   Run it from a terminal:
     epher "epher scripts/finance/investing/payback-period.epher"

   The knobs to edit are in the knob section; everything after it is
   machinery you can leave alone.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- knobs ------------------------------------------------------------------
const invested = 100000     // the project's cost today, USD
const yearly = {30000, 40000, 40000, 30000}   // inflows at the end of years 1 to 4, USD

// ---- helpers ----------------------------------------------------------------
def cum(k) = if k == 1 then yearly[1] - invested else cum(k - 1) + yearly[k]  // cumulative cash flow through year k

// ---- results -----------------------------------------------------------------
// Cumulative cash flow through each year: still 70000 short after year 1,
// 30000 short after year 2, positive from year 3 on.
for k in 1 to 4 do {k, cum(k)}
// Count the full years: the loop state is {year, cumulative cash flow} and
// runs until the cumulative total turns positive.
v = {0, 0 - invested}
while v[2] < 0 do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + yearly[v[1] + 1]}
// The payback is the last full year plus the fraction of the next year
// needed to cover the shortfall: 2 + 30000/40000 = 2.75 years.
print("payback period:", (v[1] - 1) + (yearly[v[1]] - v[2]) / yearly[v[1]], "years")
/* ---- expected output ----
= 100000
= {30000, 40000, 40000, 30000}
= {{1, -70000}, {2, -30000}, {3, 10000}, {4, 40000}}
= {0, -100000}
= payback period: 2.75 years
*/
